===Noun===
{{is-noun|@@}}

# [[horse]]
#: {{syn|is|hross|drösull<l:poetic>|drasill<l:archaic,poetic>|fákur<l:poetic>|jór<l:poetic>}}
#* Revelation 6-11 ([http://www.biblegateway.com/resources/readingplans/index.php/2010/12/29?plan=2&version=31 English] and [http://www.biblegateway.com/resources/readingplans/index.php/2010/12/29?plan=2&version=18 Icelandic])
#*: {{quote|is|Og ég sá, og sjá: Bleikur '''hestur''', og sá er á honum sat, hann hét Dauði, og Hel var í för með honum. Þeim var gefið vald yfir fjórða hluta jarðarinnar, til þess að deyða með sverði, með hungri og drepsótt og láta menn farast fyrir villidýrum jarðarinnar.|I looked, and there before me was a pale '''horse'''! Its rider was named Death, and Hades was following close behind him. They were given power over a fourth of the earth to kill by sword, famine and plague, and by the wild beasts of the earth.}}
# {{lb|is|gymnastics}} [[horse]], [[pommel horse]], [[vaulting horse]]

====Usage notes====
* The noun {{m|is|hestur}} is the ''de facto'' noun used to determine the case of another noun by substitution.
*: Using the noun {{m|is|maður||a man}} as an example:
*:: {{ux|is|ég þekki '''mann'''|I know a '''man'''|inline=1}} → {{ux|is|ég þekki '''hest'''|I know a '''horse'''|inline=1}}
*: since {{m|is|hest}} is known to be the accusative form of {{m|is|hestur}} it may be deduced that {{m|is|mann}} is in the accusative case as well.
* A commonly taught [[mnemonic]] to find the cases of nouns is reciting a set of prepositions followed by an agreeing noun and {{m|is|hestur}} is often used as an example:
*: ''[[nominative case|nominative]]'': {{ux|is|hér er '''hestur'''|there is a '''horse''' here|inline=1}}
*: ''[[accusative case|accusative]]'': {{ux|is|um '''hest'''|about a '''horse'''|inline=1}}
*: ''[[dative case|dative]]'': {{ux|is|frá '''hesti'''|from a '''horse'''|inline=1}}
*: ''[[genitive case|genitive]]'': {{ux|is|til '''hests'''|to a '''horse'''|inline=1}}

